# React and TypeScript Development Conventions

## Code Style and Linting

### ESLint Configuration
This project uses **Airbnb style guide** with TypeScript support.

**Configuration**: `.eslintrc.json`

### Indentation Rules
**CRITICAL**: Use **TABS**, not spaces!

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT
export function Component() {
	return (
		<div>
			<p>Content</p>
		</div>
	);
}

// ❌ WRONG (spaces)
export function Component() {
    return (
        <div>
            <p>Content</p>
        </div>
    );
}
```

### Key ESLint Customizations
- **Indentation**: Tabs with size 1 for switch cases
- **Max length**: Disabled (no line length limit)
- **JSX spreading**: Allowed (`react/jsx-props-no-spreading: off`)
- **Array index keys**: Allowed (`react/no-array-index-key: off`)
- **Danger HTML**: Allowed (`react/no-danger: off`)
- **jsx-a11y**: Some rules disabled (alt-text, anchor-has-content, control-has-associated-label)

### Running Linter
```bash
npm run lint  # Lints src, i18n, docs
```

**ALWAYS** run lint before committing changes.

## React Component Patterns

### SSR Safety Pattern

**CRITICAL**: All browser-dependent components MUST check for window existence!

Docusaurus uses server-side rendering during build. Components that use browser APIs (`window`, `document`, `canvas`, etc.) will crash the build if not protected.

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT: SSR-safe component
export default (props) => {
	if (typeof window !== 'undefined') {
		return <ComponentRenderer {...props} />;
	}
	return null;
};

// ❌ WRONG: Will crash during build
export default (props) => {
	const canvas = document.createElement('canvas'); // ERROR!
	return <ComponentRenderer {...props} />;
};
```

**Pattern Explanation**:
- Outer function checks `typeof window !== 'undefined'`
- Returns `null` during SSR
- Only renders actual component in browser

### CSS Modules Pattern

**ALWAYS** use SCSS modules for component styling.

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT
import styles from './Component.module.scss';

export function Component() {
	return <div className={styles.container}>...</div>;
}

// ❌ WRONG: Global classes
export function Component() {
	return <div className="container">...</div>;
}
```

**File Naming**: `ComponentName.module.scss`

### Lazy Loading Pattern

Use `@docusaurus/react-loadable` for code splitting, especially for locale-specific components.

```tsx
import Loadable from '@docusaurus/react-loadable';

const LocaleComponent = Loadable({
	loader: () => import('./ComponentCS'),
	loading: () => null,
});
```

## TypeScript Conventions

### Type Definitions
- **Location**: `src/types/` for shared types
- **Components**: Define props types inline or in same file
- **Strict mode**: Enabled in `tsconfig.json`

### Module Aliases
Use `@site/` alias for imports from project root:

```tsx
import APHCanvas from '@site/src/APHCanvas.tsx';
import config from '@site/web.config.js';
```

### Type Safety
- **Always** define prop types for components
- Use `any` sparingly (existing in APHCanvas for examples)
- Prefer interfaces over types for object shapes

## Component File Structure

### Standard Component File
```tsx
// Imports
import React from 'react';
import styles from './Component.module.scss';

// Types
type ComponentProps = {
	title: string;
	optional?: boolean;
};

// Component implementation
class ComponentRenderer extends React.Component<ComponentProps> {
	// Component logic
}

// SSR-safe export
export default (props: ComponentProps) => {
	if (typeof window !== 'undefined') {
		return <ComponentRenderer {...props} />;
	}
	return null;
};
```

## APHCanvas Component Usage

The `APHCanvas` component is the core integration point for interactive Pixi.js examples.

### Basic Usage
```tsx
import APHCanvas from '@site/src/APHCanvas.tsx';

<APHCanvas 
	name="ExampleClassName"
	width={800}
	height={600}
/>
```

### Available Props
```tsx
type APHCanvasProps = {
	name: string;              // Required: Example class name
	secondCanvas?: boolean;
	resizeToScreen?: boolean;
	transparent?: boolean;
	backgroundColor?: number;
	antialias?: boolean;
	width?: number;
	height?: number;
	resolution?: number;
	gameLoopThreshold?: number;
	gameLoopFixedTick?: number;
	speed?: number;
	canvasId?: string;
};
```

### How It Works
1. Loads examples from `static/examples/examples` bundle
2. Sets `window.BASE_URL = '../../../../examples'`
3. Requires examples bundle (once, cached)
4. Instantiates example class from `window.APH[name]`
5. Calls `example.init(canvasElement)` on mount
6. Calls `example.destroy()` on unmount
7. Blocks arrow keys and space for canvas interaction

### Example Integration Pattern
```tsx
// In a Markdown/MDX file
---
title: Example Page
---

import APHCanvas from '@site/src/APHCanvas.tsx'

## Interactive Example

<APHCanvas 
	name="SteeringSeek"
	width={800}
	height={600}
	backgroundColor={0x1a1a2e}
/>
```

## React Hooks vs Class Components

### Current Pattern
The project primarily uses **class components** for stateful components (see `APHCanvas.tsx`).

### When to Use Each
- **Class components**: For lifecycle methods, complex state management
- **Functional components**: For simple, presentational components
- **Hooks**: Acceptable for new components, but maintain consistency with existing code

## Event Handling

### Keyboard Events in Canvas
`APHCanvas` implements keyboard blocking for game controls:

```tsx
preventKeyboard = (e: KeyboardEvent) => {
	switch (e.keyCode) {
		case 37: case 39: case 38: case 40: // Arrow keys
		case 32: e.preventDefault(); break; // Space
		default: break;
	}
};
```

**Pattern**: Prevent default for arrow keys and space to avoid page scrolling during gameplay.

## Component Refs

Use refs for canvas and DOM elements that need direct access:

```tsx
class ComponentRenderer extends React.Component {
	myRef: any;
	
	constructor(props) {
		super(props);
		this.myRef = React.createRef();
	}
	
	componentDidMount() {
		// Access DOM element
		const element = this.myRef.current;
	}
	
	render() {
		return <canvas ref={this.myRef} />;
	}
}
```

## Error Handling

### ESLint Disable Comments
Use sparingly and only when necessary:

```tsx
/* eslint-disable react/require-default-props */
/* eslint-disable @typescript-eslint/no-unsafe-call */
```

**Prefer**: Fix the underlying issue rather than disabling rules.

## Performance Considerations

### Avoid Unnecessary Re-renders
- Use `React.memo` for functional components that render often
- Implement `shouldComponentUpdate` in class components if needed
- Lazy load heavy components with `Loadable`

### Canvas Optimization
- Reuse canvas instances (don't recreate on every render)
- Properly cleanup in `componentWillUnmount`
- Avoid unnecessary state updates during animation loops

## Common Pitfalls

### ❌ Don't: Use browser APIs without SSR check
```tsx
// Will crash build
const width = window.innerWidth;
```

### ✅ Do: Check for window first
```tsx
const width = typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window.innerWidth : 1024;
```

### ❌ Don't: Use global CSS classes
```tsx
<div className="container">
```

### ✅ Do: Use CSS modules
```tsx
import styles from './styles.module.scss';
<div className={styles.container}>
```

### ❌ Don't: Forget to cleanup
```tsx
componentDidMount() {
	window.addEventListener('resize', this.handleResize);
}
// Missing cleanup!
```

### ✅ Do: Remove listeners on unmount
```tsx
componentWillUnmount() {
	window.removeEventListener('resize', this.handleResize);
}
```

## Testing Changes

### Local Development
```bash
# Start development server (Czech)
npm run start_cs

# Start development server (English)
npm run start_en

# Navigate to http://localhost:3000
```

### Before Committing
1. Run `npm run lint` and fix any errors
2. Test both locales if changes affect localization
3. Test interactive examples if modifying APHCanvas
4. Check browser console for errors
5. Verify SSR safety (check build succeeds)

### Build Test
```bash
# Test build process
npm run build_cs
npm run build_en

# Serve built site
npm run serve
```
